## Why This Is Asked

This question checks practical low-level memory management fundamentals in a multithreaded context.

## Key Concepts

- Thread-local freelists and embedded per-block next pointers
- 64-byte alignment and arena initialization
- Contention avoidance without global locks and basic fragmentation awareness

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct Block {
  struct Block* next;
} Block;

typedef struct Arena {
  Block* blocks;
  size_t count;
} Arena;

static __thread Block* freelist_head = NULL;

void init_arena(Arena* a, void* mem, size_t blocks) {
  a->blocks = (Block*)mem;
  a->count = blocks;
  for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < blocks; ++i) {
    a->blocks[i].next = &a->blocks[i+1];
  }
  a->blocks[blocks - 1].next = NULL;
  freelist_head = a->blocks;
}

void* tl_alloc() {
  Block* b = freelist_head;
  if (!b) return NULL;
  freelist_head = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}

void tl_free(void* p) {
  ((Block*)p)->next = freelist_head;
  freelist_head = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle cross-thread freelist sharing to reduce fragmentation and improve memory reuse?
- What ABI- or allocator-related tests would you add to catch use-after-free or double-free bugs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental low-level concurrency concepts in a realistic, portable setup: a bounded, multi-producer, single-consumer ring with ABA avoidance and proper memory ordering. The problem also invites discussion of wrap-around, backpressure, and stress testing.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic fetch_add for tail and per-slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance via generation counters in slots
- Release/acquire semantics to order writes/reads
- Correct wrap-around handling and full/empty detection

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code sketch (not complete, for illustration)
struct Slot { uint64_t seq; uint8_t data[64]; };
Slot buffer[N];
_Atomic uint64_t tail = 0;
_Atomic uint64_t head = 0;

void* log_dequeue(void* out, size_t* len) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_load(&head);
  uint64_t pos = h & (N-1);
  if (buffer[pos].seq != h+1) return NULL;
  memcpy(out, buffer[pos].data, 64);
  *len = 64;
  atomic_store_explicit(&buffer[pos].seq, h+N, memory_order_release);
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, h+1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return out;
}

int log_enqueue(const void* msg, size_t len) {
  if (len > 64) return -1;
  uint64_t t = atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1);
  uint64_t pos = t & (N-1);
  while (buffer[pos].seq != t) _mm_pause();
  memcpy(buffer[pos].data, msg, 64);
  atomic_store_explicit(&buffer[pos].seq, t+1, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

- Follow-up: discuss how to test for starvation, memory-order pitfalls, and cache-line alignment.

## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

